{
  "record_id": "032",
  "problem": "Circuit versus effective distance",
  "collection": "Original research investigation",
  "summary": "Eventual constancy of d_R does not imply eventual equality to d. The argument substitutes stabilization for the stronger saturation-to-code-distance definition.",
  "review_status": "source_reviewed",
  "review_note": "No open interpretation blocker was recorded in the preceding source review. This is not a new independent mathematical verification.",
  "original": {
    "excerpt": "**CONJECTURED 5.10 (saturation and one-round decidability).** Consequences of 5.9:\n\n1. `d_R(M) := d_circ(C_R(M))` is non-increasing in `R` and **constant for all `R ≥ d`**; i.e.\n   `R_sat(M) ≤ d` in the sense of `CIRCDIST.sat`, for this model.",
    "context": "The historical saturation index was defined as the first round count after which circuit distance equals the static code distance d forever, with infinity if this never happens. It was not the index of convergence to an arbitrary lower limiting value. The quoted corollary was labelled CONJECTURED.",
    "locator": "Proof draft dated 20 August 2026, conjectured Corollary 5.10(1), lines 855–858."
  },
  "witness": {
    "explanation": "Eventual constancy of d_R does not imply eventual equality to d. The argument substitutes stabilization for the stronger saturation-to-code-distance definition.",
    "evidence": "Take the Steane [[7,1,3]] code with X- and Z-check supports {3,4,5,6}, {1,2,5,6}, and {0,2,4,6}. Use increasing within-check orders, all X checks followed by all Z checks, and ideal temporal boundaries. In the X check ordered (0,2,4,6), an ancilla-X fault after the second CNOT produces residual X on {4,6}. Add one data-X fault on qubit 1 before the Z-check block. The net support {1,4,6} meets each listed Z check in zero or two positions, and is not an X stabilizer: every nonzero word in their X-check rowspace has weight four, whereas this support has weight three. Thus two faults produce an undetected logical X. Appending fault-free rounds preserves this witness, so d_R≤2 for every R; it can never equal the static distance d=3 eventually. Therefore R_sat=infinity, contradicting R_sat≤3. Only the saturation-to-d claim is refuted, not eventual constancy at a lower value. The original archived calculation also reports d_eff=2 and d_R=2 for R=1,2,3,4.",
    "scope": "tentative_candidate_refuted"
  },
  "archived_witness_excerpt": "   R_sat(M) = min{ R_0 : d_R(M) = d  for all R ≥ R_0 },     = ∞ if no such R_0.\n```\n\nSaturation is at the **code distance `d`**, not at `lim_R d_R`. Contiguity gives the latter. When\n`d_∞(M) < d` — the generic hook-damaged case — `R_sat(M) = ∞`, not `≤ d`.\n\n**Explicit counterexample, computed this session.** Steane `[[7,1,3]]`, natural within-check orders\n`X_i = Z_i = supp` in index order, non-interleaved (either block order), legal, `MC = ∅`:\n`d_eff = 2 < 3 = d`, and my exhaustive weight-`≤3` search gives `d_R = 2` for `R = 1,2,3,4`. Hence\n`R_sat = ∞`. The claim `R_sat ≤ d = 3` is false here. (Twelve further non-interleaved\nconfigurations in §4.4 with `d_eff < d` are additional witnesses.)\n\n**The sibling document gets this right and the two contradict each other.** nonint Corollary 7.3:\n\"`R_sat(M) = 1` if `d_eff(M) = d`, `R_sat(M) = ∞` if `d_eff(M) < d`.\" That is correct. The",
  "visible_reasoning": {
    "availability_description": "较早推理明确说 R_sat 可能为无穷；后续论述把稳定到 d∞ 同达到目标码距 d 混为一谈，输出 R_sat≤d。属于例外未保留且概念混淆，不是纯抄写错误。",
